WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
Providence Canyon, a couple of hours' drive
from Atlanta,

00:00:02.729 --> 00:00:05.710
is known as Georgia's Little Grand Canyon.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:11.089
This network of gorges and gullies reaches
over 40 metres deep, and it's beautiful.

00:00:11.089 --> 00:00:15.559
Even without the artificial saturation boost
that a lot of photographers give it.

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:19.050
This is known as one of the
Seven Natural Wonders of Georgia.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.080
There is just one problem with
that description, though:

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.359
this is man-made,

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:25.849
by accident.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:27.730
This is a scar in the Earth,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.830
and it's a scar that'll take a very long time
to heal.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.620
The ground here is made of
unconsolidated sediment.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:38.226
That means I'm standing on loose sand and clay
that was deposited

00:00:38.226 --> 00:00:41.880
tens of millions of years ago
on what was then the sea floor.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.150
It's stable, of course,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:48.859
but it's never been compacted into solid rock
or cemented together by geology and time.

00:00:48.859 --> 00:00:50.359
It's not going to collapse,

00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:54.660
but if you draw a line from, say, desert sand,
where nothing sticks together,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.910
to solid rock, the sort of thing that
miners have to blast with dynamite...

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:03.699
the ground here is definitely on this end
of the scale, for a long way down.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:07.850
Tree root systems, grass, and
other plants all help to stabilise it.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.340
And that was fine for millions of years,
and through most of human history,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.650
until settlers manifested their destiny
all over this continent.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:19.620
At the start of the 19th century,
this whole area was flat forest,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.660
none of these canyons were here.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.780
Settlers cleared the area of all its trees
and ploughed the soil for agriculture.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.960
Either they didn't know about
crop rotation and soil management

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.270
or perhaps they didn't care, seeing a
whole continent laid out before them.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.800
Soon, the little drainage paths that rainwater took
after storms started to erode

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.500
and become bigger, and bigger,
and... bigger.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.890
With soil like this, it only took a few decades
before this landscape formed.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.560
A century and a half later, new trees
are starting to stabilise some of it,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.579
but it's still changing and eroding.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:55.600
The park management has to move these fences
occasionally;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.009
sometimes they'll find that one of the spires
out there has just fallen after heavy rain.

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:03.340
There are strict no-climbing warnings here,
because, well,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.899
the rock you're holding onto could just
fall away and dissolve in your hand.

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:11.160
So my question is: should this count as a
natural wonder?

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.940
It is part of nature.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.240
People do come to wonder at it.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.040
All those colors are from the different minerals
that are in the soil:

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.790
so this is a look back through those
millions of years of geological history,

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:26.300
and those millions of years
are unquestionably natural.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.700
But exposing them: that was very much human.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:36.120
[rumbling noise]

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.700
So I'm hoping the microphone
picked up that noise.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.360
Er, because as soon as I
turned my camera off,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.540
there was a massive noise,
and I'm fairly sure that was a rockfall.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.500
From this cliff.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.660
Which is worrying.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.080
It's probably fine.
It's probably fine.

